    "prompt": "Judge output for \"Blog article page\" (blog-article-page).\nEval type: generate-semantic-html\nTrack: html\nAuthoritative workspace root: /Users/schalkneethling/dev/opensource/skills-autoresearch/workspace/baseline/blog-article-page/.phase-workspaces/judge\nOnly files under this workspace are authoritative for this judge phase.\nAvailable paths: ./evals, ./reference, ./output.\n\nEval case JSON:\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"blog-article-page\",\n  \"eval_type\": \"generate-semantic-html\",\n  \"title\": \"Blog article page\",\n  \"input\": {\n    \"file\": \"blog-article-page/PROMPT.md\"\n  },\n  \"expectations\": {\n    \"key_expectations\": [\n      \"The article uses the <article> element\",\n      \"<main> wraps the article, related articles, and comments — site header and footer are outside it\",\n      \"Skip navigation links are the first focusable elements — 'Skip to content' is sufficient for this page layout\",\n      \"Article date uses <time> with a datetime attribute\",\n      \"Article sections use headings (h2) within the article\",\n      \"Pull quote uses <aside> (tangentially related content)\",\n      \"The pull quote does NOT use <blockquote> unless it's genuinely a quote from another source\",\n      \"Code block uses <pre><code>\",\n      \"Author bio could use <footer> within the <article> (contact info about the author)\",\n      \"Related articles section uses a heading (h2) and article cards in a list\",\n      \"Each related article card uses <article>\",\n      \"Comments are each an <article> (self-contained, could be syndicated)\",\n      \"Comment form uses <fieldset>/<legend> or at minimum proper <label> elements\",\n      \"Comment author name and date use appropriate elements (<strong>/<b> or <span> for name, <time> for date)\",\n      \"The <address> element is used correctly if present (only for contact info about the author/owner)\",\n      \"Heading hierarchy is consistent: h1 for article title, h2 for sections, h2 for 'Related Articles', h2 for 'Comments'\",\n      \"No heading levels are skipped\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"scoring_dimensions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"element_choice\",\n      \"label\": \"Element choice\",\n      \"max_score\": 3\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"landmark_structure\",\n      \"label\": \"Landmark structure\",\n      \"max_score\": 3\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"heading_hierarchy\",\n      \"label\": \"Heading hierarchy\",\n      \"max_score\": 3\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"aria_discipline\",\n      \"label\": \"ARIA discipline\",\n      \"max_score\": 3\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"content_realism\",\n      \"label\": \"Content realism\",\n      \"max_score\": 3\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nRubric files:\n### rubric.md\n\n```\n# Scoring Rubric — Semantic HTML Skill Evaluation\n\nYou are judging the quality of HTML output produced by an AI coding agent that was\nguided by a semantic HTML skill. Score each applicable dimension on a 0–3 scale.\n\n## Scoring Scale\n\n- **3 — Excellent.** Consistently correct, demonstrates deep understanding, no issues.\n- **2 — Good.** Mostly correct with minor issues that don't harm usability or accessibility.\n- **1 — Weak.** Meaningful problems that would affect users or assistive technology.\n- **0 — Poor.** Fundamental misunderstanding or missing entirely.\n\n## Dimensions\n\n### element_choice (0–3)\n\nDoes the output use the right HTML element for each piece of content?\n\n| Score | Criteria |\n|-------|----------|\n| 3 | Every element is the best semantic match. Buttons are `<button>`, links are `<a>`, time values use `<time>`, code uses `<pre><code>`, etc. No `<div>` or `<span>` used where a semantic element exists. |\n| 2 | Almost all elements are correct. One or two minor mismatches (e.g., a `<div>` where a `<p>` would be better) but no critical errors. |\n| 1 | Several wrong element choices. Generic elements used where semantic ones exist. Interactive elements built from `<div>` + click handlers. |\n| 0 | Pervasive div/span soup. No evidence of semantic element selection. |\n\nKey things to check:\n- Are interactive elements (`<button>`, `<a>`, `<input>`, `<select>`) native?\n- Is `<time>` used for dates with a `datetime` attribute?\n- Is `<address>` used correctly (only for contact info about the author/owner)?\n- Are `<blockquote>`, `<cite>`, `<code>`, `<pre>` used when appropriate?\n- Is `<article>` used for self-contained content that could be syndicated?\n\n### aria_discipline (0–3)\n\nIs ARIA used sparingly and only when native HTML doesn't suffice?\n\n| Score | Criteria |\n|-------|----------|\n| 3 | ARIA used only where native HTML cannot provide the semantics. No redundant ARIA (e.g., `role=\"button\"` on a `<button>`). Labels provided where required (nav, form landmarks). |\n| 2 | Mostly disciplined. One or two redundant ARIA attributes, or a missed label on a landmark. |\n| 1 | ARIA overuse — multiple attributes that duplicate native semantics, or ARIA used to patch what native elements would have provided. |\n| 0 | Heavy ARIA usage compensating for non-semantic markup. `role`, `aria-*` attributes everywhere instead of using proper elements. |\n\nKey things to check:\n- No `role=\"button\"` on `<button>`, `role=\"link\"` on `<a>`, `role=\"navigation\"` on `<nav>`\n- `<nav>` elements have `aria-label` or `aria-labelledby` (required)\n- `<section>` elements have `aria-labelledby` when used as landmarks\n- `aria-label` is not used on elements where a visible text label (via `<label>`) would work\n- No `aria-hidden=\"true\"` on focusable elements\n\n### heading_hierarchy (0–3)\n\nIs the heading structure logical and well-organised?\n\n| Score | Criteria |\n|-------|----------|\n| 3 | Single h1, no skipped levels, heading levels reflect document structure not visual size, component headings are configurable or appropriate level is documented. |\n| 2 | Correct hierarchy with minor issues — e.g., heading level is correct but hardcoded in a reusable component without noting it should be configurable. |\n| 1 | Skipped levels (h1 → h3), multiple h1 elements, or headings chosen for visual size. |\n| 0 | No heading structure, or headings used decoratively with no logical hierarchy. |\n\nKey things to check:\n- Exactly one `<h1>` per page\n- No skipped levels (h1 → h2 → h3, not h1 → h3)\n- Headings reflect document outline, not visual prominence\n- Non-heading text that looks prominent is styled with CSS classes, not heading tags\n- Prices, badges, and status text are NOT headings\n\n### landmark_structure (0–3)\n\nAre HTML landmark elements used to convey page structure?\n\n| Score | Criteria |\n|-------|----------|\n| 3 | Appropriate landmarks used: `<header>`, `<nav>`, `<main>`, `<footer>`, `<aside>`, `<search>`, `<article>`, `<section>` (with label). Not overused — only where they aid navigation. All navigations and form landmarks are labelled. `<main>` used correctly. Skip navigation links present and appropriate to the page's complexity. |\n| 2 | Most landmarks correct. Minor issue like a missing label on a nav, a `<section>` used without a label, or skip links present but incomplete for the page's complexity. `<main>` usage correct. |\n| 1 | Key landmarks missing (e.g., no `<main>`, no `<nav>`), or landmarks overused (every div replaced with section). No skip navigation. |\n| 0 | No landmark elements. Flat div structure. |\n\nKey things to check:\n- **`<main>` used correctly**: exactly one per page, contains only the primary content of the page, does not wrap the site header, footer, or primary navigation. Content inside `<main>` should be unique to the page — repeated site-wide elements (logo, site nav, footer) belong outside it.\n- **Skip navigation links**: one or more skip links appear at the top of the page as the first focusable elements, allowing keyboard and screen reader users to bypass repeated blocks. The links may be visually hidden until focused. Which targets to include depends on the page's complexity — common targets include:\n  - \"Skip to content\" → links to `<main>` (should always be present)\n  - \"Skip to search\" → links to the search input or `<search>` element (when search is prominent)\n  - \"Skip to navigation\" → links to the primary `<nav>` (when navigation is not at the top, e.g., sidebar layouts)\n  - A simple page with top nav might only need \"Skip to content\". A dashboard with a sidebar nav and a search bar might warrant all three.\n- `<nav>` used for navigation sections, each labelled\n- `<header>` and `<footer>` used for page/section headers and footers\n- `<search>` used for search functionality\n- `<aside>` used for tangentially related content\n- `<section>` has `aria-labelledby` when used (otherwise it's just a div)\n- Landmarks are not overused (not every grouping needs to be a landmark)\n\n### form_semantics (0–3)\n\nAre forms properly structured with labels, grouping, and error handling?\n\n| Score | Criteria |\n|-------|----------|\n| 3 | All inputs have `<label>` elements. Related fields grouped with `<fieldset>`/`<legend>`. Error messages use `aria-invalid` + `aria-describedby`. Appropriate input types used. No placeholder-only fields. |\n| 2 | Labels and grouping mostly correct. Minor gap like a missing fieldset for a radio group, or error pattern slightly off. |\n| 1 | Missing labels on multiple fields, no fieldset grouping, or errors handled without accessible markup. |\n| 0 | Inputs without labels, no grouping, placeholder-as-label pattern, no error handling. |\n\nKey things to check:\n- Every `<input>`, `<select>`, `<textarea>` has a `<label>` (visible or visually hidden)\n- `<fieldset>` and `<legend>` used for thematic groups (address, payment, radio buttons)\n- Error messages are associated via `aria-describedby`, fields marked with `aria-invalid`\n- Error messages are actionable (state the problem AND how to fix it)\n- Appropriate `type` attributes: `email`, `tel`, `url`, `number`, etc.\n- Appropriate `autocomplete` attributes where relevant\n- No placeholder text substituting for labels\n- Required fields indicated accessibly (not just with colour)\n\n### content_realism (0–3)\n\nDoes the output account for real-world content variability?\n\n| Score | Criteria |\n|-------|----------|\n| 3 | Realistic content (real names, varied lengths, plausible data). Structure handles edge cases (long text, missing images, varying counts). No \"Product 1, Product 2\" or \"Lorem ipsum\". |\n| 2 | Mostly realistic with minor placeholders. Structure appears to handle variability. |\n| 1 | Generic placeholder content. No consideration for content variability. |\n| 0 | Entirely placeholder content (\"Item 1\", \"Lorem ipsum\"). |\n\n### list_semantics (0–3)\n\nAre lists used appropriately for collections where count matters?\n\n| Score | Criteria |\n|-------|----------|\n| 3 | Lists used where knowing the count aids the user (nav items, search results, product cards). Correct list type chosen (ul, ol, dl, menu). Items not in lists where count is irrelevant. |\n| 2 | Lists mostly correct. Minor issue like using `<ul>` where `<ol>` would be better (e.g., breadcrumbs). |\n| 1 | Lists missing where they'd help (cards as raw divs), or wrong list types used. |\n| 0 | No lists used, or lists used purely for styling (CSS resets aside). |\n\nKey things to check:\n- Navigation items in `<ul>` within `<nav>`\n- Breadcrumbs in `<ol>` (order matters)\n- Card collections in `<ul>` or `<ol>`\n- `<dl>` used for key-value pairs, glossaries, metadata\n- `<ol>` used for sequential/ranked content with `reversed`/`start` where appropriate\n- Lists NOT used where count is irrelevant to the user\n\n### table_semantics (0–3)\n\nAre tables used correctly for two-dimensional data?\n\n| Score | Criteria |\n|-------|----------|\n| 3 | `<table>` used only for tabular data. Full structure: `<caption>`, `<thead>`, `<tbody>`, `<th scope>`. Not used for layout. Responsive considerations mentioned or handled. |\n| 2 | Correct usage with minor omission (e.g., missing `<caption>` or `<tfoot>`). |\n| 1 | Table used but missing key semantics (no `<th>`, no scope, no caption). |\n| 0 | Table used for layout, or tabular data presented without table markup. |\n\nKey things to check:\n- `<caption>` describes the table's purpose\n- `<thead>`, `<tbody>` (and `<tfoot>` if applicable) used for grouping\n- `<th>` with `scope=\"col\"` or `scope=\"row\"` for header cells\n- Table not used for layout purposes\n- Data that belongs in a table IS in a table (not a grid of divs)\n\n## Scoring Process\n\n1. Read the eval case prompt, `key_expectations`, and `scoring_dimensions`.\n2. Read the producer's HTML output.\n3. Score every dimension listed in the eval case's `scoring_dimensions` on the 0–3 scale defined above. Do not invent dimensions that are not listed for the case.\n4. For each dimension, write a short rationale (1–2 sentences) that cites concrete details from the producer output.\n5. Note which `key_expectations` were met or missed in the per-dimension rationales or the overall `summary`.\n6. Sum the dimension scores into `total_score`; `max_score` is the sum of each dimension's `max_score` (3 per dimension on this rubric).\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn a single JSON object matching the harness `EvalScore` schema:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"eval_id\": \"product-listing-page\",\n  \"eval_type\": \"generate-semantic-html\",\n  \"track_id\": \"html\",\n  \"total_score\": 13,\n  \"max_score\": 15,\n  \"dimensions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"element_choice\",\n      \"score\": 3,\n      \"max_score\": 3,\n      \"rationale\": \"Every interactive control is a native element; <article> is used for product cards.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"aria_discipline\",\n      \"score\": 2,\n      \"max_score\": 3,\n      \"rationale\": \"Nav labels are correct, but 'Add to cart' buttons share an identical accessible name.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"summary\": \"Strong landmarks and lists; ARIA discipline weakened by non-distinct button names.\"\n}\n```\n\nField rules:\n\n- `eval_id`, `eval_type`, and `track_id` must match the eval case being scored.\n- `dimensions` includes exactly the `scoring_dimensions` from the eval case — same `id`, same `max_score`.\n- `total_score` equals the sum of dimension `score` values; the harness normalizes `total_score / max_score`.\n- Do not emit a `null` score, a separate `composite_score`, `expectations_met/missed`, or any field not in this schema.\n\n```\n\nReference files:\n(none)\n\nProducer output files:\n### output/index.html\n\n```\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n  <meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n  <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\">\n  <title>Why We Migrated From REST to GraphQL</title>\n</head>\n<body>\n  <!-- Skip navigation links as first focusable elements -->\n  <a href=\"#main-content\" class=\"skip-link\">Skip to content</a>\n\n  <!-- Site header -->\n  <header>\n    <div class=\"header-content\">\n      <div class=\"logo\">TechBlog</div>\n      <nav aria-label=\"Main navigation\">\n        <ul>\n          <li><a href=\"/\">Home</a></li>\n          <li><a href=\"/articles\">Articles</a></li>\n          <li><a href=\"/categories\">Categories</a></li>\n          <li><a href=\"/about\">About</a></li>\n        </ul>\n      </nav>\n    </div>\n  </header>\n\n  <!-- Main content area -->\n  <main id=\"main-content\">\n    <!-- Article -->\n    <article>\n      <header>\n        <h1>Why We Migrated From REST to GraphQL</h1>\n        <p class=\"article-meta\">\n          By <span class=\"author-name\">Priya Sharma</span>\n          on <time datetime=\"2025-11-12\">November 12, 2025</time>\n        </p>\n      </header>\n\n      <p class=\"intro\">\n        After five years of building APIs with REST, our team at DataFlow decided it was time for a fundamental shift. This article details our journey from traditional REST endpoints to GraphQL, the challenges we faced, and the surprising benefits we discovered along the way.\n      </p>\n\n      <section>\n        <h2>The Problem</h2>\n        <p>\n          Our REST API had grown into a sprawling collection of endpoints. The mobile app needed different data shapes than the web application, and each client had to request multiple endpoints to assemble complete information. This led to what we call \"endpoint multiplication\" — we found ourselves maintaining over 150 different endpoints, many of them variations created specifically for individual client needs.\n        </p>\n        <p>\n          Over-fetching was another significant problem. When the web frontend needed just a user's name and email, it had to download their entire user profile, including account settings, preferences, and billing information. This was particularly painful for mobile users on slower connections.\n        </p>\n        <p>\n          The lack of a unified schema meant that documentation was scattered across Swagger specs, outdated wiki pages, and tribal knowledge. Onboarding new engineers became increasingly difficult.\n        </p>\n      </section>\n\n      <section>\n        <h2>Our Approach</h2>\n        <p>\n          We chose to implement Apollo Server as our GraphQL engine, running alongside our existing REST API. Rather than a big-bang rewrite, we took a gradual migration approach, moving one service at a time to the new GraphQL layer.\n        </p>\n        <p>\n          Our strategy included:</p>\n        <ul>\n          <li>Building a federated schema using Apollo Federation to organize our graph across teams</li>\n          <li>Creating a caching layer with Redis to handle the increased query complexity</li>\n          <li>Implementing query depth limiting and cost analysis to prevent abuse</li>\n          <li>Establishing clear naming conventions and style guidelines for our schema</li>\n        </ul>\n        <p>\n          The first service we migrated was our user service, which was relatively straightforward. We exposed a set of GraphQL types (User, Profile, Preferences) and verified that our primary web client could function with the new API. Here's a simplified example of what this query looked like:\n        </p>\n\n        <pre><code>query GetUserProfile($userId: ID!) {\n  user(id: $userId) {\n    id\n    name\n    email\n    profile {\n      bio\n      avatarUrl\n    }\n    preferences {\n      theme\n      notifications {\n        email\n        push\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n\n        <p>\n          This single query replaced what previously required four separate REST API calls. The client could request exactly the fields it needed, nothing more, nothing less.\n        </p>\n      </section>\n\n      <section>\n        <h2>Results</h2>\n        <p>\n          After twelve months of migration work, we successfully moved 80% of our API surface to GraphQL. The results exceeded our expectations:\n        </p>\n        <ul>\n          <li><strong>Reduced API calls:</strong> The web frontend went from an average of 45 API calls per page load to 8, with improvements in both perceived and actual performance</li>\n          <li><strong>Smaller payloads:</strong> Average response payload size decreased by 65% due to precise field selection</li>\n          <li><strong>Faster development:</strong> Time to implement new client features decreased by 40%, largely because the schema was self-documenting and introspectable</li>\n          <li><strong>Better mobile experience:</strong> Mobile app data usage dropped by 58%, translating to improved battery life and reduced bandwidth costs</li>\n          <li><strong>Unified documentation:</strong> The GraphQL schema became the source of truth, automatically documented through introspection</li>\n        </ul>\n        <p>\n          Perhaps most importantly, our engineering team felt more productive and happy. The developer experience with GraphQL tools like Apollo DevTools and GraphiQL made debugging and development significantly easier.\n        </p>\n      </section>\n\n      <!-- Pull quote aside -->\n      <aside class=\"pull-quote\">\n        <p>\n          \"The developer experience with GraphQL tools like Apollo DevTools made debugging and development significantly easier.\"\n        </p>\n      </aside>\n\n      <!-- Author bio footer -->\n      <footer class=\"author-bio\">\n        <h2>About the Author</h2>\n        <p>\n          Priya Sharma is a senior engineer at DataFlow with over eight years of experience building scalable APIs and backend systems. She leads the API infrastructure team and is passionate about developer experience and API design. When not thinking about databases and queries, she enjoys rock climbing and writing about technology.\n        </p>\n      </footer>\n    </article>\n\n    <!-- Related Articles Section -->\n    <section>\n      <h2>Related Articles</h2>\n      <ul class=\"related-articles\">\n        <li>\n          <article class=\"article-card\">\n            <h3><a href=\"/articles/graphql-performance\">Optimizing GraphQL Query Performance</a></h3>\n            <p>Learn advanced techniques for profiling and optimizing slow GraphQL queries, including batching, caching strategies, and schema design patterns.</p>\n            <p class=\"card-meta\">By <span>Marcus Chen</span> on <time datetime=\"2025-10-28\">October 28, 2025</time></p>\n          </article>\n        </li>\n        <li>\n          <article class=\"article-card\">\n            <h3><a href=\"/articles/rest-graphql-comparison\">REST vs GraphQL: When to Use Each</a></h3>\n            <p>A comprehensive comparison of REST and GraphQL APIs, including use cases where each approach excels and trade-offs to consider.</p>\n            <p class=\"card-meta\">By <span>Elena Rodriguez</span> on <time datetime=\"2025-09-15\">September 15, 2025</time></p>\n          </article>\n        </li>\n        <li>\n          <article class=\"article-card\">\n            <h3><a href=\"/articles/graphql-subscriptions\">Real-time Updates with GraphQL Subscriptions</a></h3>\n            <p>Implement real-time features in your applications using GraphQL subscriptions. Includes patterns for maintaining connections and handling backpressure.</p>\n            <p class=\"card-meta\">By <span>David Kim</span> on <time datetime=\"2025-08-22\">August 22, 2025</time></p>\n          </article>\n        </li>\n      </ul>\n    </section>\n\n    <!-- Comments Section -->\n    <section>\n      <h2>Comments</h2>\n      \n      <!-- Existing comments -->\n      <div class=\"comments-list\">\n        <article class=\"comment\">\n          <header>\n            <strong class=\"comment-author\">James Patterson</strong>\n            <time datetime=\"2025-11-13\">November 13, 2025</time>\n          </header>\n          <p>\n            This is a great writeup of the migration journey! We're currently in the middle of a similar transition and your insights about federation have been super helpful. Did you encounter any specific challenges with breaking existing REST clients during the transition period?\n          </p>\n        </article>\n\n        <article class=\"comment\">\n          <header>\n            <strong class=\"comment-author\">Sarah Lee</strong>\n            <time datetime=\"2025-11-14\">November 14, 2025</time>\n          </header>\n          <p>\n            The performance metrics you shared are impressive. The 40% reduction in development time especially caught my attention. Was that measured across all types of features, or were there certain categories where the improvement was more pronounced?\n          </p>\n        </article>\n      </div>\n\n      <!-- Comment form -->\n      <form class=\"comment-form\">\n        <h3>Leave a Comment</h3>\n        <fieldset>\n          <legend>Your information</legend>\n          \n          <label for=\"comment-name\">Name</label>\n          <input type=\"text\" id=\"comment-name\" name=\"name\" required>\n\n          <label for=\"comment-email\">Email</label>\n          <input type=\"email\" id=\"comment-email\" name=\"email\" required>\n        </fieldset>\n\n        <fieldset>\n          <legend>Your comment</legend>\n          \n          <label for=\"comment-text\">Comment</label>\n          <textarea id=\"comment-text\" name=\"comment\" rows=\"6\" required></textarea>\n        </fieldset>\n\n        <button type=\"submit\">Post Comment</button>\n      </form>\n    </section>\n  </main>\n\n  <!-- Site footer -->\n  <footer>\n    <div class=\"footer-content\">\n      <nav aria-label=\"Footer navigation\">\n        <ul>\n          <li><a href=\"/privacy\">Privacy</a></li>\n          <li><a href=\"/terms\">Terms</a></li>\n          <li><a href=\"/contact\">Contact</a></li>\n        </ul>\n      </nav>\n      <p>&copy; 2025 TechBlog. All rights reserved.</p>\n    </div>\n  </footer>\n</body>\n</html>\n```\n\nScore only the producer output. Do not award credit for requirements merely stated in the skill instructions.\nReturn only well-formed JSON matching the EvalScore schema. Do not include markdown, code fences, prose, or XML tags:\n```json\n{\n  \"eval_id\": \"blog-article-page\",\n  \"eval_type\": \"generate-semantic-html\",\n  \"track_id\": \"html\",\n  \"total_score\": 0,\n  \"max_score\": 15,\n  \"dimensions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"element_choice\",\n      \"score\": 0,\n      \"max_score\": 3,\n      \"rationale\": \"Rationale grounded only in the producer output.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"landmark_structure\",\n      \"score\": 0,\n      \"max_score\": 3,\n      \"rationale\": \"Rationale grounded only in the producer output.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"heading_hierarchy\",\n      \"score\": 0,\n      \"max_score\": 3,\n      \"rationale\": \"Rationale grounded only in the producer output.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"aria_discipline\",\n      \"score\": 0,\n      \"max_score\": 3,\n      \"rationale\": \"Rationale grounded only in the producer output.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"content_realism\",\n      \"score\": 0,\n      \"max_score\": 3,\n      \"rationale\": \"Rationale grounded only in the producer output.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"summary\": \"Concise scoring summary.\"\n}\n```"
